{
  "info": {
    "name": "XYZ Bank — BFFs",
    "description": "End-to-end requests for the three channel BFFs against the local compose stack (`docker compose up --build`), using each channel's real credential instead of trusted headers.\n\n**Before running anything:**\n1. Generate the dev certs: `./scripts/generate-dev-tls-certs.sh` (from the repo root).\n2. Postman → Settings → General → turn OFF \"SSL certificate verification\" (the dev CA is self-signed; Postman collections can't embed a custom trust store).\n3. For the **bff-atm** folder only: Postman → Settings → Certificates → Add Certificate for host `localhost` port `8083`, PFX file `dev/certs/atm-terminal/keystore.p12`, passphrase `xyzbank-dev`. Every bff-atm request needs this client certificate (mTLS) or it will be rejected at the TLS layer before reaching the app.\n4. For **config-server / eureka / interests-service**: run the `interests-service` folder after `docker compose up --build`. Those requests are plain HTTP on ports 8888 / 8761 / 8084 — no TLS.\n\n**bff-web and bff-mobile logins are not exercisable from Postman**: both go through an OIDC redirect to a provider that, in this repo, only exists as a JUnit/WireMock test fixture (see the root README) — there's nothing at the identity-provider end for Postman to redirect to. Their folders instead use a pre-request script that mints a session/device JWT locally, signed with the same dev secret every service already trusts (`channel-auth.jwt.secret`, default `dev-channel-auth-jwt-signing-secret-please-rotate-in-prod`). This is a dev-only shortcut to exercise the *domain* endpoints end-to-end without a real login — it never touches core-service's actual login/refresh machinery, and only works because that signing secret is a well-known local default. If you've overridden `CHANNEL_AUTH_JWT_SECRET` in your environment, update the `jwtSecret` collection variable to match.\n\n**bff-atm's flow is fully real end-to-end**: PIN verification against the seeded demo card issues a genuine 120-second session from core-service; everything downstream uses that real session, no shortcuts.\n\n**Interest apply note:** the demo seed already has a 2025 annual summary. Use `applyYear=2026` (collection default) for `POST .../interest-applications` so core does not reject a duplicate year.",
    "schema": "https://schema.getpostman.com/json/collection/v2.1.0/collection.json"
  },
